Here is the cleaned-up and improved code. Beyond fixing formatting, this fixes a subtle bug in your original code: df was created when reading the CSV, but the subsequent lines referenced data instead of df (which would cause a NameError).

Python
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import pandas as pd

# 1. Load data
file_path = '/Users/summercollier/OEAS805/HW2/International_Coastal_Cleanup_20260908.csv'
df = pd.read_csv(file_path)

# 2. Select columns to analyze
cols = [
    'Cigarette Butts',
    'Plastic Pieces',
    'Food Wrappers (candy, chips, etc.)',
    'Bottle Caps (Plastic)',
    'Beverage Bottles (Plastic)',
    'Straws, Stirrers',
]

# 3. Clean numeric data (remove commas and handle non-numeric values)
df[cols] = df[cols].apply(
    lambda x: pd.to_numeric(x.astype(str).str.replace(',', ''), errors='coerce')
)

# 4. Calculate total counts and sort
top_debris = df[cols].sum().sort_values()

# 5. Plot data
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4.5))
top_debris.plot(kind='barh', color='coral', ax=ax)

# 6. Customize labels and layout
ax.set_title('Top Debris Types', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold', pad=12)
ax.set_xlabel('Total Collected', fontsize=11)
ax.set_ylabel('')  # Removes redundant y-axis label

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()
What was improved:
Fixed Variable Name: Changed data[cols] to df[cols] so the DataFrame matches the variable name initialized during pd.read_csv().

Removed Hidden Characters: Removed non-breaking space characters (\xa0) present in the original string list that can trigger SyntaxError.

Explicit Plotting (Object-Oriented API): Switched to plt.subplots() and ax=ax. This is recommended PEP 8 practice and avoids unexpected layout overlaps when running multiple cells in Jupyter.

Readable Multi-Line Formatting: Split the cols list and long function call onto separate lines for better readability.

Cleaned Y-Axis: Added ax.set_ylabel('') so matplotlib doesn't display an auto-generated label over your category names.
